Real code:

public interface IGameObserver {
    void onScoreChanged(int newScore);
    void onGameOver(int finalScore);
}

// SnakeGame
private final List<IGameObserver> observers = new ArrayList<>();

public void addObserver(IGameObserver observer) { observers.add(observer); }

private void notifyScoreChanged() {
    for (IGameObserver observer : observers) {
        observer.onScoreChanged(score);
    }
}

notifyScoreChanged() loops over a list and calls the same method on each entry. What does SnakeGame know about who is in that list?,"Their concrete types, so it can call the right method on each","Only that each one implements IGameObserver - it does not know how many there are, what they are, or what any of them does with the score",It knows their order of registration matters,It knows at least one must be a ConsoleScoreObserver,,2,"That ignorance is the point. Adding a listener that writes to a file, or a network socket, requires no change here because there is nothing here to change.",2

Real code:

public interface IGameObserver {
    void onScoreChanged(int newScore);
    void onGameOver(int finalScore);
}

// SnakeGame
private final List<IGameObserver> observers = new ArrayList<>();

public void addObserver(IGameObserver observer) { observers.add(observer); }

private void notifyScoreChanged() {
    for (IGameObserver observer : observers) {
        observer.onScoreChanged(score);
    }
}

// SnakeGame.performMove(), inside the eatsFood branch
score += scoringRule.pointsFor(currentFood);
currentFood = spawnFood();
notifyScoreChanged();

notifyScoreChanged() is called only inside the eatsFood branch, not on every move. What does that mean for a listener?,Listeners are told about every move and must filter out the ones they do not care about,Listeners hear only when the score actually changed - a move that just slides the snake forward produces no notification at all,Listeners are told once per second,Listeners are told only when the game ends,,2,In the recorded run this is why only seven score lines printed across roughly thirty moves. An event stream that fires when nothing changed forces every listener to write the same filtering code.,1

Real code:

public class HighScoreObserver implements IGameObserver {

    @Override
    public void onScoreChanged(int newScore) {
        // high score only matters once the game ends
    }

    @Override
    public void onGameOver(int finalScore) {
        HighScoreRegistry.getInstance().recordScore(finalScore);
    }
}

HighScoreObserver implements onScoreChanged() with an empty body and a comment. Is an empty implementation a code smell here?,Yes - it means the interface is wrong and should be split,"It is a mild one. This listener only cares about one of the two events, so the interface is slightly wider than it needs. Splitting it would remove the empty method but double the number of interfaces to register against",No - empty methods are always fine,Yes - it should throw UnsupportedOperationException instead,,2,"Worth putting to the class as a trade-off rather than a verdict. With two events an empty method is cheap

Real code:

public interface IGameObserver {
    void onScoreChanged(int newScore);
    void onGameOver(int finalScore);
}

// SnakeGame
private final List<IGameObserver> observers = new ArrayList<>();

public void addObserver(IGameObserver observer) { observers.add(observer); }

private void notifyScoreChanged() {
    for (IGameObserver observer : observers) {
        observer.onScoreChanged(score);
    }
}

public class HighScoreObserver implements IGameObserver {

    @Override
    public void onScoreChanged(int newScore) {
        // high score only matters once the game ends
    }

    @Override
    public void onGameOver(int finalScore) {
        HighScoreRegistry.getInstance().recordScore(finalScore);
    }
}

public class ConsoleScoreObserver implements IGameObserver {
    @Override
    public void onScoreChanged(int newScore) {
        System.out.println("score: " + newScore);
    }
}

Both listeners are registered on the same game and both receive every event. How much does each know about the other?,Each holds a reference to the other so they can coordinate,"Nothing at all - neither can see the other, and removing either one would have no effect on the remaining one's behaviour",The console listener must be registered first or the high score will be wrong,They share state through HighScoreRegistry,,2,"Independence is the practical test of whether this is really working. If the printing listener had to be present for the high score to record correctly, they would not be independent at all.",2

Real code:

public class HighScoreRegistry {

    private static final HighScoreRegistry INSTANCE = new HighScoreRegistry();

    private int highScore = 0;

    private HighScoreRegistry() { }

    public static HighScoreRegistry getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }

    public void recordScore(int score) {
        if (score > highScore) {
            highScore = score;
        }
    }
}

The instance is created in a static final field rather than lazily inside getInstance() with a null check. What does that change?,Nothing - both approaches behave identically in every respect,"The object is created once when the class first loads, so getInstance() is only a return with no branch - it cannot ever construct a second one or be caught halfway through constructing the first",It means the registry can be garbage collected between calls,It allows getInstance() to take constructor arguments,,2,"Deliberately different from VendingMachine.getInstance(), which uses the lazy null check that class-30's notes had already flagged. Same pattern, tightened after the lesson.",2

Real code:

public class HighScoreRegistry {

    private static final HighScoreRegistry INSTANCE = new HighScoreRegistry();

    private int highScore = 0;

    private HighScoreRegistry() { }

    public static HighScoreRegistry getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }

    public void recordScore(int score) {
        if (score > highScore) {
            highScore = score;
        }
    }
}

In the recorded stretch-goal run, the first game scored 27 and a second game played in the same run scored 18. The final line printed 'all-time high score: 27'. Which two behaviours does that single line confirm?,Only that the second game scored less than the first,"That the registry persisted across both games rather than resetting, and that recordScore() keeps the maximum rather than the latest value",That the high score is stored per game and the highest is printed at the end,That the second game failed to record its score at all,,2,"Both halves of the requirement in one number: shared across games, and a maximum rather than a running value. Had it printed 18, the second would have been wrong

Real code:

public class HighScoreRegistry {

    private static final HighScoreRegistry INSTANCE = new HighScoreRegistry();

    private int highScore = 0;

    private HighScoreRegistry() { }

    public static HighScoreRegistry getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }

    public void recordScore(int score) {
        if (score > highScore) {
            highScore = score;
        }
    }
}

// requirement, from requirements.txt section 9
// The game must remain correct if a second game is created and played in the same
// program run, with no state leaking between them (except the shared all-time high score)

The requirement forbids state leaking between games but explicitly allows this one exception. What makes the high score different from the score, the snake body or the current condition?,"It is an int, and primitives cannot leak",It is a property of the player across all their games rather than a property of any one game - so it is the one value whose whole purpose is to outlive a single game,It is different because it is stored in a singleton,It is not really different

