public class HighScoreObserver implements IGameObserver {

    @Override
    public void onScoreChanged(int newScore) {
        // high score only matters once the game ends
    }

    @Override
    public void onGameOver(int finalScore) {
        HighScoreRegistry.getInstance().recordScore(finalScore);
    }
}

MilestoneObserver was added later, announcing when a score crosses a threshold. Which existing classes had to change to support it?,"IGameObserver, to add an onMilestone() method","None - it implements the existing interface and is registered with addObserver(), so SnakeGame and the other two listeners were untouched","SnakeGame, to call the new listener at the right moment","HighScoreRegistry, to track the milestone",,2,The third listener cost one new file and one registration line. That is the concrete version of 'a new score listener must be addable in isolation'.,1

Real code:

public class MilestoneObserver implements IGameObserver {

    private final int milestone;
    private boolean announced = false;

    @Override
    public void onScoreChanged(int newScore) {
        if (!announced && newScore >= milestone) {
            announced = true;
            System.out.println("  [milestone] passed " + milestone + " points");
        }
    }
}

Unlike the other two listeners, this one has a mutable 'announced' field. What must a caller therefore avoid doing with it?,Nothing - listeners may always be shared,"Registering the same instance on two different games, since the flag would already be set from the first game and the second game's milestone would never be announced",Registering it before the game starts,Registering it alongside HighScoreObserver,,2,The same trap as the counting spawn strategy: a stateful helper reused across games silently carries its history. Stateless listeners can be shared freely

Real code:

private void endGame() {
    setState(GameOverState.getInstance());
    notifyGameOver();
}

public interface IGameObserver {
    void onScoreChanged(int newScore);
    void onGameOver(int finalScore);
}

// SnakeGame
private final List<IGameObserver> observers = new ArrayList<>();

public void addObserver(IGameObserver observer) { observers.add(observer); }

private void notifyScoreChanged() {
    for (IGameObserver observer : observers) {
        observer.onScoreChanged(score);
    }
}

onGameOver() is delivered from endGame(), which also sets GameOverState. If instead each of the two death paths in performMove() called notifyGameOver() itself, what could go wrong?,Nothing - the result would be the same,"One path could set the state and forget to notify, or notify without setting the state, and a future third death path would have to remember to do both",The listeners would be called twice for one death,The state would change before the score was calculated,,2,Funnelling both endings through one private method makes the pairing structural rather than something each call site has to remember.,2

Real code:

public interface IGameObserver {
    void onScoreChanged(int newScore);
    void onGameOver(int finalScore);
}

// SnakeGame
private final List<IGameObserver> observers = new ArrayList<>();

public void addObserver(IGameObserver observer) { observers.add(observer); }

private void notifyScoreChanged() {
    for (IGameObserver observer : observers) {
        observer.onScoreChanged(score);
    }
}

There is addObserver() but no removeObserver(). What does that prevent, and does it matter for this program?,It prevents any listener from being registered twice,"A listener cannot be detached once added, so a temporary one - say, a tutorial hint that should stop after the first food - has no way to unsubscribe. It does not matter today because every listener lives for the whole game",It prevents the observer list from ever being read,It means listeners are notified in random order,,2,"Reasonable to leave out until something needs it. Worth naming as a deliberate omission rather than an oversight, and worth noting that a real removeObserver has to cope with removal during notification.",2

Real code:

public interface IGameObserver {
    void onScoreChanged(int newScore);
    void onGameOver(int finalScore);
}

// SnakeGame
private final List<IGameObserver> observers = new ArrayList<>();

public void addObserver(IGameObserver observer) { observers.add(observer); }

private void notifyScoreChanged() {
    for (IGameObserver observer : observers) {
        observer.onScoreChanged(score);
    }
}

private void notifyGameOver() {
    for (IGameObserver observer : observers) {
        observer.onGameOver(score);
    }
}

Suppose a listener's onGameOver() threw an exception. What happens to the listeners registered after it?,"They are still notified, because each listener runs independently","They are never called - the exception propagates out of the loop, so one badly behaved listener can silently starve every listener behind it",The exception is caught and logged automatically,The game restarts,,2,"A plain loop makes every listener depend on the good behaviour of the ones before it. Wrapping each call in a try/catch is the usual fix, and worth asking students whether the failure should be swallowed or collected.",2
